NAME
       sensor-charter - operator-facing declaration of rights, responsibilities,
                        and safe-use principles for RTT sensor systems

SYNOPSIS
       The sensor charter defines:
           • operator rights when using RTT sensor systems
           • operator responsibilities during sensor operation
           • platform and constellation guarantees
           • safety-first principles for overlays, clarity, and fusion
           • quick-reference rules for hazard conditions

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-charter provides a concise, operator-focused summary of the
       rights, responsibilities, and guarantees that govern the use of RTT
       sensor systems. It is designed for real-time decision-making on the
       bridge, in mission control, or during autonomous operations.

       The charter ensures:
           • operators understand what the sensor stack owes them
           • operators understand what they owe the system
           • safe, predictable behavior during high-sensitivity modes
           • clarity during anomalies, storms, and turbulence
           • alignment with the sensor constitution

OPERATOR RIGHTS
       Operators are guaranteed the following rights:

           Right to Clarity
                  Sensor outputs must be clear, stable, and free of distortion.

           Right to Truthful Data
                  Overlays and clarity filters must not conceal hazards.

           Right to Early Warning
                  Anomalies, storms, and turbulence must be reported promptly.

           Right to Safe Synthesis
                  Fusion modes must not exceed safe thresholds.

           Right to Calibration Transparency
                  Operators may view calibration status and drift indicators.

           Right to Override Unsafe States
                  Operators may command rollback to safe modes.

           Right to Lineage Access
                  Operators may inspect sensor history and decision lineage.

OPERATOR RESPONSIBILITIES
       Operators must uphold the following responsibilities:

           Responsibility to Monitor Drift
                  Watch φ_harm, spectral variance, and sync jitter indicators.

           Responsibility to Respect Thresholds
                  Do not force overlays or clarity modes beyond safe limits.

           Responsibility to Respond to Alerts
                  Act on anomaly, storm, and turbulence warnings.

           Responsibility to Maintain Sync
                  Avoid maneuvers that destabilize constellation coherence.

           Responsibility to Preserve Lineage
                  Do not erase or bypass sensor history.

           Responsibility to Report Irregularities
                  Notify governance systems of unexpected sensor behavior.

PLATFORM GUARANTEES
       Platforms guarantee:

           • stable overlay baselines  
           • calibrated clarity filters  
           • harmonic-lock protection  
           • anomaly/storm/turbulence detection  
           • safe synthesis fallback modes  
           • sync-field integrity  

CONSTELLATION GUARANTEES
       Constellations guarantee:

           • unified sensor standards  
           • cross-platform fusion  
           • shared hazard maps  
           • sync-field coherence  
           • distributed storm/turbulence forecasting  

SAFETY-FIRST PRINCIPLES
       Operators must follow these principles at all times:

           1. Clarity before action
                  Ensure clarity filters are stable before interpreting data.

           2. Fusion follows stability
                  Do not engage deep synthesis during drift or jitter.

           3. Hazard overrides preference
                  Storm and anomaly protocols supersede operator mode choices.

           4. Sync is sacred
                  Never compromise constellation sync for local clarity.

           5. Lineage is law
                  All sensor decisions must be recorded.

QUICK-REFERENCE RULES
       For rapid use during operations:

           • φ_harm jitter > 0.05/cycle → unsafe  
           • spectral variance +50% → turbulence likely  
           • polarity inversion → gradient hazard  
           • sync jitter spike → constellation risk  
           • broadband turbulence + harmonic collapse → storm precursor  

       Immediate actions:
           • activate clarity filters  
           • reduce synthesis depth  
           • isolate affected bands  
           • notify WRSADC  
           • prepare hazard navigation mode  

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • enforces charter rights  
           • monitors operator responsibilities  
           • manages hazard protocols  
           • maintains sync-field integrity  
           • blocks unsafe sensor states  

       WRSADC is the charter guardian.

NOTES
       The sensor charter is the operator-facing companion to the sensor
       constitution. It provides a concise, practical framework for safe,
       transparent, and effective use of the RTT sensor stack.

